    "durationMs": 192038,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. Summary of what I built:\n\n**Stack:** Express 4.21.2 (pinned exactly) + `pg` (idiomatic Node/Postgres persistence layer), Node 22.\n\n**Structure:**\n- `src/app.js` — all routes: `POST /links` (auto or custom codes, 201/409/422), `GET /links/:code` (200/404/422), `GET /r/:code` (302 + Location, atomic `UPDATE ... clicks + 1`), `GET /links` (`{items, total}` in creation order). Malformed JSON is caught by a custom error handler and answered 422; all validation failures return 422 JSON with an `error` field.\n- `src/db.js` — Pool reading `DATABASE_URL` from the environment, nothing hard-coded.\n- `src/migrate.js` + `migrations/001_create_links.sql` — SQL-file migrations tracked in a `schema_migrations` table, so only pending ones apply on each start.\n- `start.sh` — applies pending migrations, then `exec`s the server in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000` (no dependency installation inside).\n- `README.md` — includes a \"Starting the server\" section.\n\n**Verification:** a 46-assertion smoke suite passed (auto/custom codes, duplicate → 409, all 422 validation cases including malformed JSON, 302 redirect with exact Location header, click incremented exactly once, list ordering and totals). I restarted the server mid-test and confirmed links and click counts survive; the migration ledger correctly skips already-applied migrations on restart. The database is left clean, and `sh start.sh` was verified end-to-end three times, including a final clean-state run."
  }
